h **...g6** (since moving the King is impossible or leads to immediate loss, and no other piece can block).\n3. White then plays **Nxg6**. This Knight move attacks the Rook on h8, the Queen on d8, and threatens discovered checks. \n4. If Black captures the Knight with **...hxg6** (the only pawn defending g6), White follows with **Qxg6#** (Checkmate), as the King has no escape squares and the Queen is safe.\n\nOther moves like N: e5-f7 are inferior because the Knight is undefended and can simply be captured by the King (Kxf7), leaving White down material without a forced win.\n\nReason: Q: d1-h5 initiates a forced mating sequence (or wins decisive material) by exploiting the weak/missing f7 pawn and the exposed position of the Black King.\n\nAnswer: Q: d1-h5"}
